<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shōgun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seii Taishogun, shogun, shogunate, shogunal, Barbarian-Subduing Generalissimo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shōgun was the title of the military rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, short for Seii Taishōgun, meaning barbarian-subduing generalissimo. Shoguns held real power while the emperor retained ceremonial authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> ruled Japan from the early seventeenth century until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, when the shogunate was abolished and power returned to the emperor, as accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> note.</p></div>
